	"notes": "Ars Technica's front page keeps its masthead and every row of story cards inside the same centred column, about 1152px wide, no matter how wide the browser gets — an ultrawide monitor just gets bigger empty margins on both sides, the same trick as several other sites in this batch. What makes this one worth a second look is a control bar just under the masthead offering four named view modes — Classic, Grid, List, and something called Neutron star — plus a 'Grid settings' button. That is the clearest possible example of the owner's point that one page's appearance can be styled multiple ways: instead of the CSS quietly switching layouts at a breakpoint, this site lets the reader pick the layout by hand, and what this record describes is only the 'Grid' mode. On a phone, everything — the two-up featured story, the three-up card rows — drops to one plain column.",
